                <h2>Why Spreadsheets Can't Manage Three Platforms: The Case for a Systematic Dashboard</h2>
<p>Teams running overseas advertising typically manage multiple platforms simultaneously—Meta, Google, TikTok, and more. Account balances, spending records, top-up history, and invoice status are scattered across various chat windows and spreadsheets. Operations teams spend 2-3 hours weekly on reconciliation, only to find mismatches. The root cause isn't carelessness—it's <strong>information fragmentation and lack of a unified view</strong>.</p>
<p>A systematic dashboard doesn't just solve "whether something was recorded"—it enables "can I see the big picture at a glance." This guide provides a practical checklist for building an agency account management dashboard.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Operations Checklist: Building a Systematic Agency Account Dashboard</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Define Dashboard Users and Core Requirements</h3>
<p>Before building, answer three questions:</p>
<ul>
<li>Who will use this dashboard? Operations, media buyers, finance, and management each need different data views.</li>
<li>What's the core problem to solve? Balance alerts, top-up tracking, spending monitoring, or multi-account comparison?</li>
<li>Where does the data come from? Manual entry, platform API integration, or agency portal export?</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Checklist:</strong> Dedicated views for each role ✓ | Core data dimensions defined ✓ | Data sources confirmed ✓</p>
<hr />
<h3>Step 2: Design the Account Master Table Structure</h3>
<p>The foundation of your dashboard is a well-structured account master table. Suggested fields:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Field</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Platform</td>
<td>Meta / Google / TikTok etc.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Account ID</td>
<td>Platform-assigned account number</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Account Name</td>
<td>Business-friendly identifier</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Account Type</td>
<td>Agency account / Direct client</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Balance</td>
<td>Current available balance</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Alert Threshold</td>
<td>Minimum balance before alert</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Account Status</td>
<td>Active / Restricted / Suspended</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Owner</td>
<td>Sales/Operations person in charge</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Created Date</td>
<td>For lifecycle tracking</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Checklist:</strong> Fields cover core operational data ✓ | Real-time status updates ✓ | Filtering and bulk operations supported ✓</p>
<hr />
<h3>Step 3: Build Top-up and Spending Flow Views</h3>
<p>This is where most issues arise. Bind top-up records with spending data bidirectionally.</p>
<p><strong>Top-up Flow Checklist:</strong><br />
- Client submits top-up request ✓<br />
- Operations confirms amount and target account ✓<br />
- Finance reviews payment proof ✓<br />
- Agency confirms receipt ✓<br />
- Account balance updated ✓<br />
- Top-up reference number and timestamp recorded ✓<br />
- Top-up completion notification sent ✓</p>
<p><strong>Reconciliation Flow Checklist:</strong><br />
- Pull platform spending reports on fixed weekly schedule ✓<br />
- Compare against system records ✓<br />
- Flag anomalies (e.g., midnight spikes, threshold breaches) ✓<br />
- Generate discrepancy report ✓<br />
- Annotate problematic accounts for follow-up ✓</p>
<hr />
<h3>Step 4: Configure Balance and Top-up Alerts</h3>
<p>One key value of a dashboard is <strong>proactive risk identification</strong>, not reactive damage control.</p>
<p><strong>Alert Rules Checklist:</strong><br />
- Auto-alert when single account balance drops below threshold ✓<br />
- Reminder when top-up request pending over 24 hours ✓<br />
- Alert when daily spend exceeds 200% of daily average ✓<br />
- Immediate notification when account receives policy violation warning ✓<br />
- Alert on invoice status changes (issued, pending shipment) ✓</p>
<hr />
<h3>Step 5: Conduct Regular Dashboard Reviews and Optimize</h3>
<p>The first month after launch is a calibration period. Conduct a structured monthly review:</p>
<ul>
<li>Are any fields consistently empty? Is it a habit issue or a design flaw?</li>
<li>Is alert response time meeting targets?</li>
<li>Any high-frequency manual tasks that could be automated?</li>
<li>Is finance aligned with operations on data definitions?</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Real-World Example</h2>
<p>A utility app's overseas team managed 12 Meta accounts and 5 Google accounts. Before implementing dashboard management, operations spent 40 minutes every morning manually compiling platform balances and previous-day spending, often missing top-up windows due to time zone gaps.</p>
<p>After adopting a systematic dashboard, top-up response time dropped from an average of 6 hours to 2 hours, and reconciliation errors decreased by 80%. The key change was moving the "request—review—receipt—notification" chain from WeChat conversations into the dashboard, with timestamps and owners for each step.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>Quality agency account management isn't about how many accounts you've opened—it's about <strong>whether you can manage them clearly and use them effectively</strong>. A good dashboard doesn't dump all data together; it delivers the right information to the right person at the right time.</p>
<p>If your team still manages ad accounts via spreadsheets and group chats, start today: create an account master table and build a minimum viable dashboard prototype. Get it running first, then refine.</p>
